#. %1 app name
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"[Localization](https://community.kde.org/Get_Involved/translation) - help to "
"translate %1 into your language\n"
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"[Documentation](https://community.kde.org/Get_Involved/documentation) - help "
"us improve user documentation to make %1 more friendly for newcomers\n"
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"[Promotion](https://community.kde.org/Get_Involved/promotion) - help us "
"promote %1 both online and offline\n"
msgstr ""

#. %1 link
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"[Updating wiki](%1) - help us update the information present in the wiki, "
"add new tutorials, ... to make it easier for others to join!\n"
msgstr ""

#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid "Do you have any other idea? Get in touch!"
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name, %2 link
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"%1 is already available on majority of Linux distributions. It can be "
"installed directly from [Discover](%2), [GNOME Software](%2) or your "
"distribution's [software store](%2)."
msgstr ""

#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid "Release Sources"
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"%1 is released regularly as part of KDE Gear. You can find %1 latest stable "
"release among the [tarballs from the latest KDE Gear release](https://"
"download.kde.org/stable/release-service).\n"
"\n"
"If you want to build %1 from sources, we recommend checking our [Getting "
"Involved](/get-involved) page which contains links to full guide how to "
"compile %1.\n"
msgstr ""

#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid "Flatpak"
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name, %2 link, %3 link
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"You can install the latest [%1 flatpak](%2) from Flathub. Experimental "
"flatpaks with nightly builds of %1 can be [installed from the KDE Flatpak "
"repository](%3)."
msgstr ""

#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid "Installation"
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"To install %1, follow the instructions of the platform you are using for how "
"to install software. Most platforms have a graphical installer which can be "
"used to install software packages. In many cases it will automatically be "
"opened when clicking the download or install link for your platform. There "
"is a [generic tutorial for installing KDE applications](https://userbase.kde."
"org/Tutorials/Install_KDE_software) which also can be applied to %1."
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"To install %1 or one of its components as a stand-alone application, follow "
"the instructions of the platform you are using for how to install software. "
"Most platforms have a graphical installer which can be used to install "
"software packages. In many cases it will automatically be opened when "
"clicking the download or install link for your platform. There is a [generic "
"tutorial for installing KDE applications](https://userbase.kde.org/Tutorials/"
"Install_KDE_software) which also can be applied to %1."
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"If you are using Linux, %1 might already be pre-installed on your system as "
"part of a default selection. If not, you can install it with the package "
"management tool of your Linux distribution. See its documentation for "
"details."
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"In many cases you will have the opportunity to install %1 in a modular way, "
"so you can decide to only install the set of components you actually want to "
"use. Translations of the user interface often also will be available as "
"separate module you can install according to your needs and preferences."
msgstr ""

#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid "Uninstallation"
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"To uninstall %1, follow the instructions of the package management tool you "
"have used to install %1. This will remove the %1 application. It will not "
"touch data you have viewed, created, or modified with %1."
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"To uninstall %1, follow the instructions of the package management tool you "
"have used to install %1 and its components. This will remove the components "
"of your choice. It will not touch data you have viewed, created, or modified "
"with %1."
msgstr ""

#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid "Handbook"
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name, %2 link
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"Lost in %1 settings or just looking to discover more features in %1? You can "
"read the [%1 Handbook](%2) to find answers to your questions."
msgstr ""

#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid "Need Help?"
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name, %2 link
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"Check %1 on [KDE Forum](%2), maybe someone has already solved a similar "
"problem. And if not, feel free to ask there!"
msgstr ""

#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid "Found a Bug?"
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"Bugs in software happen. If you found some in %1, please report them into "
"[our Bugzilla](https://bugs.kde.org). Please do not report bugs on forums or "
"mailing lists - such reports will most likely be overlooked or forgotten by "
"the developers quite soon. To make sure the bug gets noticed by developers "
"and fixed as soon as possible, report it into Bugzilla."
msgstr ""

#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid "Missing a Feature?"
msgstr ""

#. %1 app name
#: i18n/en.yaml:0
msgid ""
"%1 has a lot of features, but there's always a room for one or two more. If "
"you have an idea for an improvement or you are missing a feature in %1, "
"write your suggestion in our [Bugzilla](https://bugs.kde.org) so that the "
"developers can find it and implement it."
msgstr ""

